1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,000
 What happens to an AI agent when the process executing it disappears? Hello, I'm Russell and welcome to episode 12 of the Articulate Journal, where I'm exploring what it takes to build an AI-native architecture from first principles. In the previous episode, I explored what AI systems mean when they talk about memory. And what initially looked like one architectural concept turned out to be several different responsibilities. Knowledge belongs to the knowledge of AI-native architecture.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 Knowledge Model, conversation has its own state. Embedings and indexes support retrieval and can usually be rebuilt.

3
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 Even much of the temporary context used while an agent is reasoning can potentially be reconstructed.

4
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 But one form of state was different. Execution. If an agent is halfway through some work and a process disappears, how does the system know where it got to?

5
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 At first, the requirement seems fairly simple. If an agent is interrupted, I want it to continue.

6
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 Maybe it's analyzing the impact of an architectural change. Maybe it needs clarification from me.

7
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 Maybe another agent needs to contribute, or perhaps it's waiting for approval.

8
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 Whatever the reason, I don't want the lifetime of that work tied to the lifetime of a thread, a container, or a particular agent instance.

9
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 If that process disappears, the work shouldn't disappear with it. But that raises another question.

10
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 Do I actually need to preserve execution? Couldn't I just run the agent again?

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 Imagine an agent analyzing the impact of a proposed architectural change.

12
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 It retrieves relevant architectural knowledge. It identifies the affected capabilities.

13
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 It looks at decisions and principles that might be relevant. Then it asks me a question before continuing.

14
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 If that process disappears at that point, perhaps nothing particularly important has been lost.

15
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
 The knowledge models still exist, conversation exists, the context can be reconstructed.

16
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
 The agent can retrieve the information again and continue reasoning.

17
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
 It might perform some work twice. Perhaps that's acceptable.

18
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 So maybe the execution state isn't particularly special after all.

19
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 The problem appears when the agent starts to do things.

20
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 Let's change the scenario slightly.

21
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
 Before asking me for approval, the agent records a proposed architectural decision.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 It invokes another capability, sends a notification asking me to review something, and then it waits.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 Now imagine the process disappears. When it starts again, they have a slightly different problem.

24
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 Was the proposed decision already recorded? Did the other capability complete?

25
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 Was the notification already sent? If I simply restart the reasoning process, should those actions happen again?

26
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
 Suddenly the problem isn't remembering what the agent knew.

27
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:14,000
 The problem is knowing what the system has already done. And that distinction matters.

28
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:22,000
 This is where execution begins to look different from the other forms of state that were explored in a previous episode.

29
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
 Knowledge can be retrieved, conversations can be replayed.

30
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
 Context can be reconstructed, and reasoning can often be performed again.

31
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 But once work has caused an effect outside the reasoning process, we need to know whether that effect has actually happened.

32
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:47,000
 The runtime needs to know what completed, what failed, which tools have already been executed, which external effects have occurred.

33
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 Isn't simply another form of memory. It's a record of progress.

34
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
 This becomes particularly important because agents don't operate in isolation.

35
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
 They call tools. They invoke other capabilities. They update systems. They request approvals.

36
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 They communicate with external services.

37
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 Every time execution crosses one of those boundaries, something may happen that can't simply be forgotten or performed again without consequence.

38
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 Something the same notification twice might only be annoying.

39
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 Creating the same record twice could be more serious.

40
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 Invoking another capability twice could cause an entire different chain of work to happen again.

41
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
 So recovering an agent isn't simply about reconstructing its prompt and asking the model to continue.

42
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 The system needs to understand what has already happened before it can safely decide what should happen next.

43
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
 And then we introduce us, human beings.

44
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
 Suppose an agent reaches a point where it needs an architect to approve something.

45
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 The response might arrive five minutes later. It might arrive tomorrow. It might arrive next week.

46
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 And it might never arrive at all.

47
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 Keeping a process alive while it waits is really the answer.

48
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 The execution needs to survive independently of the process currently performing the work.

49
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 But there's another complication. The world may have changed while the agent was waiting.

50
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
 New architectural knowledge may have been accepted.

51
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 Another decision may have been made. The proposal itself may have been superseded.

52
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:33,000
 The state the agent originally reasoned may no longer represent the current architecture.

53
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,000
 So, resuming execution isn't necessarily the same as waking up a suspended process.

54
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:44,000
 We've re-entered a system that may have changed while we were away.

55
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
 Time has become part of the architecture.

56
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
 And this is where the understanding of the problem changed.

57
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,000
 Durable execution isn't really about keeping the agent alive.

58
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
 It's about preserving the continuity of work.

59
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 We don't necessarily need to preserve every temporary thought or every piece of reasoning context.

60
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 Much of that can be reconstructed.

61
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:20,000
 What we need to preserve is enough of the execution to understand what work is completed, what work remains, what effects have already occurred.

62
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,000
 What we're waiting for and what can safely happen next.

63
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 The process performing the work may disappear. The execution should not.

64
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 The process is temporary. The execution is durable.

65
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
 This also gives me a much cleaner boundary around the runtime itself.

66
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
 In the previous episode I discovered that

67
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
 the runtime owns far less memory than I initially expected.

68
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,000
 It doesn't own architectural knowledge. That's the responsibility of the knowledge model.

69
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 It doesn't need to own every conversation. It doesn't need to treat embeddings, indexes and caches as permanent memory.

70
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,000
 Much of that context required by an agent can be reconstructed from the wider platform.

71
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 But the execution is different.

72
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:17,000
 The runtime coordinates work.

73
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:25,000
 If that work can span processes, failures, agents, external systems, humans and potentially days of elapsed time,

74
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:31,000
 then preserving the continuity of that execution becomes one of the most important responsibilities.

75
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:36,000
 That gives me another important requirement for ADR1.

76
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:42,000
 The runtime articulates, eventually uses, needs to support work that survives interruption.

77
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
 It needs pause and resume semantics. It needs to recover from failure,

78
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:51,000
 support long-running execution and support humans becoming part of that execution.

79
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:57,000
 It needs enough execution history to understand what has already happened.

80
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:04,000
 And it needs to coordinate work, and it needs to coordinate work that may be distributed across different processes

81
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
 and different points in time.

82
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:13,000
 Those aren't simply useful runtime features anymore. They've become the architectural requirements.

83
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
 Over the last three episodes, the question has gradually changed.

84
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,000
 I started asking which agent runtime articulates you to use.

85
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
 Then I had to understand what state the runtime actually owns.

86
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
 And now I've discovered one of the most important responsibilities isn't really memory at all.

87
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,000
 It's preserving work across failure and across time.

88
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 Once tools have executed, once other capabilities become involved, once humans enter the process

89
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000
 and once execution can last longer than the process performing it,

90
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
 simply starting again isn't enough. We need continuity.

91
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
 We need to know what happened, and we need to know what can safely happen again.

92
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:57,000
 That gives me the final architectural requirement I need for ADR1.

93
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,000
 Now I can go back to the candidate runtimes and evaluate them against the architecture

94
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 I'll articulate actually needs.

95
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:09,000
 And that's what we'll do for the next episode. See you on the next one.

